From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Broyojo <dha@xoba.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 15:29:42 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 2/6] hw/net/mlx5: evaluate flow steering, model flow control,
 stamp at the MAC

Receive flow steering: keep the flow tables, groups and entries the
driver programs and classify every received frame against them, starting
at the root table and following forward and miss chains.  TIRs select the
receive queue, through an RQ table indexed by a Toeplitz (optionally
symmetric) or XOR8 hash of the fields the TIR selects.  This makes MAC and
VLAN filtering, promiscuous mode, ethtool flow rules and RSS configuration
behave as on hardware, and reports the real hash and flow tag in
completions.

Flow control: with global pause enabled (the default, as on hardware), a
frame that finds its receive queue empty is left with the network backend
and retried instead of dropped, and a port stops transmitting while its
link partner cannot take frames.  The emulated link has no speed limit of
its own, so without this a sender easily overruns the receiver.

Timestamps: take the transmit timestamp when the frame is handed to the
backend and the receive timestamp on arrival, before classification and
copying, as a MAC would.  Between two back-to-back ports this brings the
gap down from tens of microseconds to a few.

Receive checksum flags now reflect actual verification of the IPv4 header
and TCP/UDP checksums.

